{
  "query": "You are the database lead at Riverbend Retail and you manage a Cloud Spanner instance that powers an order management API. During busy shopping periods you see elevated latency for reads and writes and the slow queries frequently join the Orders, Payments, Customers and Shipments tables to build order summaries. You want to refine the data model so queries complete faster while preserving consistency. What should you do?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Normalize the schema further to remove duplicate attributes",
      "explanation": "This approach focuses on eliminating redundancy by splitting data into more tables with stricter relationships",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Cloud Memorystore for Redis",
      "explanation": "This managed cache can serve frequently read keys from memory to offload the database for some requests",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Apply selective denormalization to reduce the number of joins on hot query paths",
      "explanation": "This technique stores commonly needed fields together so queries that assemble summaries can avoid multiple table joins",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Increase the number of nodes in the Cloud Spanner instance",
      "explanation": "This adds more compute and storage resources to the instance to improve capacity and parallelism",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Apply selective denormalization to reduce the number of joins on hot query paths</b>.</p><p>By copying a small set of frequently read attributes from Customers, Payments and Shipments into the Orders row or into a compact summary table, your queries can fetch everything they need with far fewer joins. In Cloud Spanner you can write the original data and the redundant summary fields in the same transaction, which preserves strong consistency while reducing the CPU and network cost of multi table joins during peak traffic.</p><p><i>Normalize the schema further to remove duplicate attributes</i> is counterproductive here because it increases the number of tables and joins. Your slow queries already span Orders, Payments, Customers and Shipments, so further normalization would likely worsen latency.</p><p><i>Cloud Memorystore for Redis</i> adds a separate cache that is not part of Spanner transactions and introduces cache invalidation complexity. It can help when some staleness is acceptable, but the requirement is to preserve consistency for order management, which makes a cache the wrong choice for this problem.</p><p><i>Increase the number of nodes in the Cloud Spanner instance</i> can raise throughput and reduce queuing under load, yet it does not address the intrinsic cost of join heavy queries. The question asks for a data model refinement, so adding capacity is not the best solution to reduce query latency caused by multiple joins.</p>",
